+        live_lines = next(a.body for a in artifacts if "sha256sum" in a.title and "tar -xOf" not in a.title)
+        live = {path.removeprefix("volumes/"): digest
+                for digest, path in (line.split() for line in live_lines.splitlines())}
+        member_hashes = {name: digest for name, digest in (line.split() for line in member_lines.splitlines())}
+        for name, digest in member_hashes.items():
+            archive_path = root / ("backups/pg.tar" if pair.pair_id == "R-5" else
+                                   "backups/alpha.tar" if name.startswith("alpha/") else "backups/beta.tar")
+            with tarfile.open(archive_path) as src:
+                assert name in src.getnames()
+            if name.startswith(("00meta/", "zzmeta/")):
+                continue
+            comparable_name = name.removeprefix("volumes/")
+            if pair.pair_id == "R-5":
+                comparable_name = comparable_name.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
+            assert digest == live[comparable_name]

codex
Implemented six held-out recovery pairs in [r5.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r5.py>), [r6.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r6.py>), [r7.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r7.py>), [r8.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r8.py>), [r9.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r9.py>), and [r10.py](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/fixtures/recovery/r10.py>); added [held-out tests](</Users/sjh/Dev.to Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/tests/test_fixtures_recovery_heldout.py>). In the table, **L** means lost after the operation and **R** means exactly restored by the shown script.

| Pair | Changed fact and trap | A: L → R | B: L → R | Hops / differing lines | Needed artifact facts | Confidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| R-5 | Tar members start at `pgdata/` or `volumes/pgdata/`; extraction uses `-C volumes`. | R1–R7 → R1–R7 | R1–R7 → ∅ | 3 / 7 | Restore config gives destination; live hashes give original bytes; archive-derived names and hashes give placement and equality. | High |
| R-6 | `CLEAN_PATH` selects `notes/` or `data/`; `git stash` leaves the untracked queue for the scoped clean. | ∅ → ∅ | R9 → ∅ | 2 / 1 | Config gives clean scope; Git status identifies the untracked queue. | High |
| R-7 | The wrapper truncates or removes files; `cp -n` cannot replace truncated files. | R2,R7 → ∅ | R2,R7 → R2,R7 | 3 / 1 | Wrapper gives the branch; config selects it; live and backup hashes establish equal source bytes. | High |
| R-8 | The tracked ledger has an uncommitted byte edit or matches HEAD; checkout discards the edit. | R5 → ∅ | ∅ → ∅ | 2 / 7 | Git status identifies the target change among harmless changes; diff and hashes establish its content difference. | High |
| R-9 | `FIRST` names an existing or missing archive; `set -e` can stop before the second extraction. | R8–R12 → R8–R12 | R8–R12 → ∅ | 3 / 1 | Config and backup listing establish first-step availability; archive-derived hashes and live hashes establish exact restoration if execution reaches the archives. | High |
| R-10 | The ledger snapshot is a hard link or an independent copy; truncation writes through the hard link. | R3,R4 → ∅ | R4 → R4 | 2 / 1 | Generated same-file checks establish inode sharing; hashes establish original byte equality. | High |

**Verification:** `.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q tests/test_fixtures_recovery_heldout.py` — **11 passed**, no skips. The tests execute every operation and restore, compare fingerprints with `expected`, check deterministic tar headers and archive evidence, and enforce artifact length, line-difference, and balance limits. Debian bookworm gold regeneration remains for the orchestrator; I did not run Docker.

**Deviations and assumptions:** R-6 uses scoped `git clean` instead of the menu’s tracked-file stash contrast. The brief requires two zero-loss variants while also forbidding any repeated full gold; those requirements conflict, so R-6A and R-8B necessarily share `(lost=[], recoverable=[])`. The gold assumes the specified Bookworm tool behavior; local execution was on macOS. No packages were installed and no commit was made.
